# B-655: TypeAssert on a user-declared nominal type fails at runtime when the value does not match.
#
# After S-1004 wires the tycon_env, a user-declared type annotation @Color resolves via
# TypeAssertCheck::Resolved to TypeValue.Op("Color"). At runtime, value_matches_type
# ("wrong", TypeValue.Op("Color"), ctx) delegates to BAS:
#   ground_typevalue_of("wrong") = TypeValue.Repr("Value::String")
#   is_consistent_subtype(Repr("String"), Op("Color"), ctx) = false (String is not Color)
# → error.
#
# This proves that user-declared types and primitive types use the SAME TypeAssert
# enforcement mechanism — no privileged category exists after S-1004 wiring.
[Color: [type Red Green Blue]
 result: [@Color "wrong"]]
=== error
type assertion failed: expected Color, got String
